WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.949
OK — so, if you’re here you probably watched
our breakdown of Technicolor.

00:00:03.949 --> 00:00:06.670
If not, I’ll put the link up here somewhere.

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:11.250
I wanted to make this video to cover some
of the things that I didn’t have time to

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:16.170
put in that video, some of the research and
footnotes, and also to thank the people who

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:17.980
helped us make that video.

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:22.380
In this case, that would be Eastman House
— their museum that you can go to, you can

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:27.080
find them online — and they were really
generous with their time, their knowledge,

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:30.350
and also a lot of the images that you see
in the video.

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:34.980
And I also want to thank the Smithsonian of
course, which let us talk to their curator

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:40.050
— also very knowledgeable — and get in
there and film that giant Technicolor camera

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:41.510
and the gorgeous ruby slippers.

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:47.630
I got to film those ruby slippers myself,
so I think, basically, I’m like a legendary

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:50.760
Hollywood cameraman now, because I’ve shot
those ruby slippers.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:52.350
Um, just a theory.

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:53.960
Anyway, let’s get to it.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:58.750
Let’s watch the video and I’ll tell you
what we had to leave out and what we really

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:01.140
wanted to put in.

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:09.060
So yeah, obviously this video is centered
around Dorothy and the Wizard of Oz.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:14.229
I have to tell you that I’m a person who
believed some of these misconceptions before

00:01:14.229 --> 00:01:16.229
I started making this video.

00:01:16.229 --> 00:01:20.810
I may have known that it wasn’t the first
Technicolor video, but I didn’t know the

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:24.719
degree to which Technicolor shaped the movie.

00:01:24.719 --> 00:01:30.240
I did think that there was some sort of technological
breakthrough there, but it was largely a marketing

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:31.240
breakthrough.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:34.939
They really sold this movie as a Technicolor
movie because it cost them a lot of money

00:01:34.939 --> 00:01:36.359
to make that way.

00:01:36.359 --> 00:01:43.219
There were other movies beforehand or in contemporaneous
production that also used Technicolor.

00:01:43.219 --> 00:01:50.899
So, this is an important thing to clear up.

00:01:50.899 --> 00:02:00.999
And this whole video just sprang from my curiosity
— why does Technicolor look like that?

00:02:00.999 --> 00:02:05.719
And it turned out to be more complex than
I thought.

00:02:05.719 --> 00:02:17.060
Yeah, so, sorry to slam the Maryland Science
Center, but what are you gonna do?

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:18.829
They said it.

00:02:18.829 --> 00:02:24.810
OK, so, please look at Barbara Flückiger
website, because it shows you how complex

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:29.819
all this color theory is and also how much
people who know about photography care about

00:02:29.819 --> 00:02:32.769
the depth and history of their discipline.

00:02:32.769 --> 00:02:36.959
There are so many little gradations that are
fascinating to look at.

00:02:36.959 --> 00:02:42.349
OK, I have to tell you one thing I left out
about Kalmus and Comstock.

00:02:42.349 --> 00:02:46.719
Before they got into Technicolor, they developed
other camera nerd projects, and one of their

00:02:46.719 --> 00:02:52.730
early projects was actually a speed camera,
like the kind that catches speeders on the

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:55.300
road, and apparently it worked.

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:59.670
This is the early 1900s, but they weren’t
able to sell it to any police departments,

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:01.700
probably because they didn’t understand
it.

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:05.400
But could have been a whole different 20th
century if they’d stuck with that speed

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:09.280
camera idea instead of moving on to Technicolor.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:11.359
Ah yes!

00:03:11.359 --> 00:03:15.780
This is Flowers and Trees which is so cool
looking.

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:22.430
I am not a gamer myself but there’s this
game out called Cuphead now that is inspired

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:26.739
by the animation styles of these 1930s Technicolor
movies.

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:31.780
You should look it up on YouTube because they
have a really good emulation of this animation

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:32.780
style.

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:37.280
It looks great and it makes you understand
animation better.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:41.019
OK, so this is my Technicolor breakdown.

00:03:41.019 --> 00:03:45.340
This is the full page of that Technicolor
diagram that I showed.

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:50.409
Now, already there’s a lot of different
methods, so when I talk about two-strip and

00:03:50.409 --> 00:03:56.659
three-strip here, I’m kind of condensing
a bunch of different processes into one most

00:03:56.659 --> 00:04:00.890
significant step from two-strip to three-strip.

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:06.200
An analogy that I thought of was if in the
year 2100, they were covering the iPhone,

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:13.450
they wouldn’t cover iPhone, iPhone 4, iPhone
4s, iPhone 5, iPhone 5s, or whatever the proper

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:18.419
gradations are, they would just say there
were Blackberries, then there were iPhones,

00:04:18.419 --> 00:04:19.419
then there were Androids.

00:04:19.419 --> 00:04:22.960
They would kind of give you the bigger picture,
and that’s what I’m doing here with Technicolor.

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:26.780
I’m kind of giving you the bigger picture
of the switch to three-strip.

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:31.580
There were all sorts of processes within that
that you can look up and see the differences

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:35.660
between them, both in the technology and in
the results.

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:44.150
And so for these simulations, it was fun because
in After Effects I went through and I did

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:52.240
actually properly tint the things with cyan,
tint the things with green, and then actually

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:54.139
layer them on top of one another.

00:04:54.139 --> 00:04:59.830
So I didn’t just replace it with the original
picture at the end, this is a proper combination

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:03.370
of all those colors, and that kinda helped
me get a grasp on it too, to have to do it

00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:05.300
the right way.

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:10.020
That helped me understand how this stuff actually
worked (with the help of the people I talked

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:14.330
to).

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:24.300
Generally, the key, which I show there, I
heard generally it stopped being used after

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:25.300
1945.

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:31.050
One of the reasons they would use it is because
of blurring, also because it was really hard

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:38.470
to get the three strips in exactly the right
place, so sometimes they’d be just a little

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:43.910
bit off — a micrometer off, or what we would
call a pixel today off — and so a key could

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:46.130
help kind of settle them into place.

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:51.819
Lock them into place over that black and white
image.

00:05:51.819 --> 00:06:02.150
Yeah, so I was amazed — Ryan at the Smithsonian
told me about this, that was the first time

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:06.940
I had heard about it and thought that was
an amazing piece of trivia there.

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:11.490
Even though they did have mattes at the time,
which is what I do here with my hand on the

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:16.970
little box, they painted this room sepia because
that was the easiest way to use the camera

00:06:16.970 --> 00:06:19.180
continuously for the shot.

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:24.220
That’s just really crazy and you can see
it if you watch it, you can see that it’s

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:27.000
a different camera filming that scene.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:35.599
Yes, and here’s the gloriously large Technicolor
camera.

00:06:35.599 --> 00:06:40.629
Ryan was super smart, really helped me understand
this process better.

00:06:40.629 --> 00:06:45.780
My greatest pleasure though was enjoying the
historical irony of filming this gigantic

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:49.320
camera with my tiny Sony camera.

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:51.580
Literally, it’s this big.

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:54.250
I’m using it to film this right now.

00:06:54.250 --> 00:06:59.729
And I’m filming this thing that the best
filmmakers in the world, in history too, had

00:06:59.729 --> 00:07:05.910
to use, and I’m able to film in 4k, grading
at will, with just this tiny little camera.

00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:10.750
So, it’s just amazing to me and bizarre
to get to do that.

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:17.080
Again these cameras were super loud — they
had to really adjust around that.

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:19.860
That’s Jimmy Stewart, clown right there.

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:24.159
The press materials say “It was always Jimmy’s
dream to play a clown.”

00:07:24.159 --> 00:07:30.780
I never knew that about him, hadn’t really
heard about that movie, even though it’s

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:31.780
famous.

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:34.900
So yeah, this is a great scene.

00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:39.650
Eastman House gave me this strip and these
prints from Singin’ in the Rain and it was

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:51.060
really fun to match them up with the scenes
in 

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:53.379
the movie.

00:07:53.379 --> 00:08:00.490
Bobbie Koshay — she’s really cool.

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:07.120
We couldn’t find a photo that we could license
in time for this video, but if you look her

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:12.870
up, you can find some pictures of her testing
out different dresses I think, and there’s

00:08:12.870 --> 00:08:19.319
also one of her with the body double for the
Scarecrow, and it’s really fun to look at

00:08:19.319 --> 00:08:24.130
and compare because Judy Garland as Dorothy
is so iconic, and then you see this other

00:08:24.130 --> 00:08:33.040
person who looks a little like her, but isn’t
her — it’s really fun to see.

00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:38.130
The slippers were originally silver — I
know that a lot of the people who think the

00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:43.070
Wizard of Oz is about the fight between the
gold and the silver standard point to these

00:08:43.070 --> 00:08:46.770
silver slippers as evidence of that theory
about the work.

00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:55.660
I asked Ryan about that — he was sort of
uncertain exactly where that theory is right

00:08:55.660 --> 00:08:56.660
now.

00:08:56.660 --> 00:09:00.720
There’s some people apparently who don’t
believe it, some who do think it’s true,

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:02.750
that it was about the fight over the gold
standard.

00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:08.310
But that was a little too much historical
baggage to weigh in on this video.

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:14.050
So we just left it out, but you can look it
up and you can find a lot of information out

00:09:14.050 --> 00:09:15.870
there.

00:09:15.870 --> 00:09:20.260
So here’s Natalie Kalmus — here I showed
the credits for Gone With the Wind.

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:24.700
She actually fought a lot on that movie because
she wanted more extreme colors.

00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:30.280
She didn’t like the muted browns that were
being used, so she could definitely be difficult

00:09:30.280 --> 00:09:34.570
to tangle with sometimes, which I think makes
her interesting, because it shows the power

00:09:34.570 --> 00:09:39.520
of the Technicolor Company that she could
go around and basically tell directors what

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:45.030
to do on their own movies, because Technicolor
was that powerful and using color was that

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:54.870
new that they were ready to believe her.

00:09:54.870 --> 00:09:57.240
And Technicolor really endured for a while.

00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:03.720
Ultimately it was ease, but it was also expense
of the process that doomed the traditional

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:08.190
process that we recognize and that we describe
here.

00:10:08.190 --> 00:10:14.140
It was just really pricey to process these
films, to do that dye transfer process, and

00:10:14.140 --> 00:10:15.390
so the company changed.

00:10:15.390 --> 00:10:20.800
Technicolor is definitely still around today,
but it is a different company and it doesn’t

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:22.680
use that three-strip process.

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:27.710
There are some minor exceptions of when the
three-strip process has been used after the

00:10:27.710 --> 00:10:33.030
Godfather Part II, which is about to come
up here — there it is — so after that,

00:10:33.030 --> 00:10:40.220
there were some minor exceptions of revivals
or restorations in which they did do a three-strip

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:42.210
process, but for the most part it’s gone.

00:10:42.210 --> 00:10:45.410
So it’s fair enough to say that it doesn’t
exist.

00:10:45.410 --> 00:10:52.160
Yeah, and then, today we have this whole world
of digital color grading — besides me complaining

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:58.360
about the dust clouds of Trapcode Particular
plugin in Stranger Things.

00:10:58.360 --> 00:11:03.770
It kind of got its start in O Brother Where
Art Thou, and in The Aviator, Martin Scorsese

00:11:03.770 --> 00:11:11.161
actually graded the scenes digitally to echo
the Technicolor process that was used in the

00:11:11.161 --> 00:11:13.630
year in which the movie was set.

00:11:13.630 --> 00:11:19.210
So he has in the early scenes of the movie
he has a two-strip looking thing; in the later

00:11:19.210 --> 00:11:22.910
years, he has a three-strip looking thing.

00:11:22.910 --> 00:11:26.940
To my modern eyes, it doesn’t look like
they did that great a job with it.

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:31.000
But that’s for your judgment whether you
think it accurately simulates the Technicolor

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:32.000
look.

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:37.100
I think because Kalmus wasn’t there and
the Technicolor wasn’t there, it’s more

00:11:37.100 --> 00:11:41.240
of an homage than an exact emulation.

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:42.240
And yeah, that’s it.

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:43.930
I’ve talked through the end of the video.

00:11:43.930 --> 00:11:48.850
It also brought us forward to this world of
digital color grading, which is what exists

00:11:48.850 --> 00:11:52.610
today, it’s what I’ll use on this video,
and it’s very different.

00:11:52.610 --> 00:11:56.790
So yeah, I just wanted to make this video
to kind of get into some of the things that

00:11:56.790 --> 00:11:58.330
we had to leave out.

00:11:58.330 --> 00:12:03.480
The world of Technicolor is way more complex
than this, and this was ten minutes long,

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:08.590
so the outro to that video was me kind of
talking through why this video had to be so

00:12:08.590 --> 00:12:09.590
long.

00:12:09.590 --> 00:12:15.830
Because I went in and I said I want to just
use After Effects to show people how three-strip

00:12:15.830 --> 00:12:20.630
and two-strip worked, and that ended up being
a chunk of it, but it was way more complex

00:12:20.630 --> 00:12:21.630
than that.

00:12:21.630 --> 00:12:22.630
Which is really cool.

00:12:22.630 --> 00:12:27.000
It’s not just a technological story, it’s
a cultural story of this company that had

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:30.900
a lot of power and these people who were learning
how to use color.

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:32.480
And that’s what I think makes it interesting.

00:12:32.480 --> 00:12:35.460
So thank you for watching.

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:39.370
Please take this opportunity to nerd out more
on Technicolor.

00:12:39.370 --> 00:12:44.260
Visit Barbara Flueckiger’s website, visit
Eastman House, both in person and online,

00:12:44.260 --> 00:12:45.790
whatever you can do.

00:12:45.790 --> 00:12:50.390
And if you can’t do that, if you aren’t
interested in learning any more Technicolor

00:12:50.390 --> 00:12:54.200
details, look up one of these old movies and
just marvel at them.

00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:59.390
Check out Singin’ in the Rain, see The Adventures
of Robin Hood, or look up some of the old

00:12:59.390 --> 00:13:00.580
cartoons.

00:13:00.580 --> 00:13:04.990
One of the slides that I have in there is
from a Woody Woodpecker cartoon that was in

00:13:04.990 --> 00:13:07.540
Technicolor, and they’re gorgeous.

00:13:07.540 --> 00:13:14.540
They really do have a unique look and a unique
use of color that is just wonderful to hang

00:13:14.540 --> 00:13:15.940
out with for a few hours.

00:13:15.940 --> 00:13:18.430
So, thanks for watching and hopefully you’ll
enjoy it.

